<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">30 September 1862 · Berlin</p><h1 class="sp-title">Bismarck: Iron and Blood</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Prussia's liberal majority in parliament had refused to pay for King Wilhelm I's enlargement and reform of the army, and the king appointed Otto von Bismarck minister-president to break the deadlock. A week into office, Bismarck told the budget committee of the lower house that Prussia's future would not be settled by parliamentary votes.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Not through speeches and majority decisions are the great questions of the day decided — that was the great mistake of 1848 and 1849 — but by iron and blood.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Speech to the budget committee of the Prussian House of Representatives, 30 September 1862, translated here.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Bismarck governed for four years without a lawful budget and fought three wars, against Denmark in 1864, Austria in 1866 and France in 1870–71. The last ended with the proclamation of the German Empire in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles in January 1871, with Wilhelm as emperor and Bismarck as chancellor.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proclamation-of-the-german-empire">Proclamation of the German Empire</a><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a></div></div></section>
